 A two-stage search algorithm to find the approximate location of the minimum value in a volume along a ray. The first stage performs a coarse linear scan, and the second stage refines the position using a divide-by-two approach. Note: despite the title, the source implementation searches for the minimum value.
 
+### Stochastic Isosurface Depth Extraction
+A technique to find the distance to the nearest isosurface intersection along a viewing ray by iteratively sampling random points closer to the camera, requiring only one volume sample per iteration [S3]. For each pixel, a ray is cast from the camera through the pixel to compute near ($n$) and far ($f$) intersections with the volume [S3]. A random value $\xi$ is generated between 0 and the current depth buffer value $G_{i,j}$ [S3]. The sample point is calculated as $t = n + \xi(f - n)$ [S3]. If the volume value at $t$ exceeds the isovalue $\rho$, the depth buffer is updated: $G_{i,j} = \xi$ [S3]. The method solves $V(n + G_{i,j} \cdot (f - n)) - \rho = 0$ [S3]. $G_{i,j}$ stores the normalized distance to the isosurface (0 for $n$, 1 for $f$) [S3]. This approach allows parallel extraction during rendering, improving interactivity [S3].
